./LOGBOOK.md:772:### 1145 — libssh2 adapter remains blocked on server-rekey observability and keepalive replies (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:773:- BLOCKER: The accepted `libssh2_session_rekey` fork clears client byte/time/manual trigger initiation only. The exact public header exports no KEX lifecycle callback or generation query. Pinned libssh2 handles inbound `SSH_MSG_KEXINIT` entirely inside private `packet.c`/`transport.c` state, so the adapter cannot truthfully emit `.serverInitiated`, mark `activeKeyExchange`, increment generation, or distinguish server-KEX success/failure through the public boundary.
./LOGBOOK.md:774:- BLOCKER: `libssh2_keepalive_send` writes `keepalive@libssh2.org` with `want_reply=1` but returns after queueing and stores no outstanding request or reply outcome. The public API exposes no generic reply-requiring global request or request-success/failure correlation. Consequently the adapter cannot measure reply RTT, enforce reply timeout/miss allowance, or distinguish acknowledgement from silence.
./LOGBOOK.md:775:- EVIDENCE: The public macOS XCFramework exports only `libssh2_session_rekey`, `libssh2_keepalive_config`, and `libssh2_keepalive_send` among relevant symbols. `keepalive.c:52-96` contains no reply wait/state; inbound server rekey is private at `packet.c:1336-1369` and `transport.c:407-425,1039-1048`. Trace callbacks are unavailable in non-debug builds and are not a supported protocol API.
./LOGBOOK.md:776:- ROUTING: Do not report these gates green or build a mock-only partial adapter. Recommend a separately reviewed minimal fork extension exposing typed server-KEX lifecycle plus a nonblocking reply-correlated global-request/keepalive API; alternatives are explicitly failing the libssh2 candidate or revising the mandatory contract. Exact options and resume decision are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_server-rekey-keepalive-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:778:### 1115 — Minimal libssh2 public client-rekey fork clears the adapter blocker (TASK-260720-3vwls7)
./LOGBOOK.md:779:- DECISION: Pinned libssh2 `a34302491c164d53c900fec9b3cbb050ecebe719` carries one allowlisted two-file patch: `include/libssh2.h` publicly declares `libssh2_session_rekey`, and `src/session.c` adds a null-checked, nonblocking-friendly `BLOCK_ADJUST` wrapper over the existing `ssh2_kex_exchange(session, 1, &session->startup_key_state)`. No crypto, algorithm, packet, channel, or private-header surface changed.
./LOGBOOK.md:781:- VERIFICATION: The public-header-only C integration test drove the wrapper through three `EAGAIN` results, observed client-initiated NEWKEYS installation, then forced OpenSSH `RekeyLimit` server-initiated NEWKEYS and proved post-rekey channels remained usable. Fork verify/rekey/source-tamper gates, Swift public-module packaging test, native seam tests, strict Swift format, 62 core tests, and `swift build` pass; `ReluxTunnelCore` remains free of libssh2/OpenSSL imports.
./LOGBOOK.md:783:### 1030 — libssh2 adapter stops on absent public client-rekey API (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
./LOGBOOK.md:784:- BLOCKER: Exact pinned archive `a34302491c164d53c900fec9b3cbb050ecebe719` reproduced SHA-256 `744ba3e9…c87c`. Public `include/libssh2.h` has no rekey operation; initial `libssh2_session_handshake` calls private `ssh2_kex_exchange(..., reexchange=0)`, while only inbound `SSH_MSG_KEXINIT` reaches private `ssh2_kex_exchange(..., reexchange=1)` from `src/packet.c`.
./LOGBOOK.md:785:- IMPACT: Mandatory byte/time/explicit client rekey and `SSHTransport.requestRekey` cannot be implemented through the pinned public API. Calling the private symbol, exporting it in-task, replaying handshake, or reporting success without KEX would violate the task's no-patch/no-hidden-fallback boundary. Product code and partial packaging stopped before implementation.
./LOGBOOK.md:786:- ROUTING: Recommend recording libssh2 red/failed at this pin. The alternative is a separately authorized, audited libssh2 fork/public rekey hook (or a future upstream pin followed by full re-audit). Exact evidence and tradeoffs are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_libssh2-rekey-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:792:- ROUTING: Recommend an accepted candidate-neutral connected-socket/engine-bootstrap seam plus ReluxNIOSSH consumer-driven receive-credit and maximum-packet/intake hooks, each with deterministic tests. Exact evidence, rejected workarounds, alternatives, and resume condition are attached as `TASK-260715-1af33i_adapter-api-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:797:- FINDING: Snapshot lifecycle is sticky-correct: `SSHConnectionStateMachine`'s own snapshot resets to nil on transition to `userAuthentication`/`active`, but `NIOSSHHandler` re-reads it after every processed message while the KEX state machine still holds `NegotiationResult`, and only overwrites on non-nil (`NIOSSHHandler.swift:684`). Consequence: during a rekey the handler snapshot updates when the new `NegotiationResult` is computed (KEXINIT processing), slightly before newKeys activate.
./LOGBOOK.md:798:- NOTE: Adapter caveats: (1) for AEAD ciphers (`aes*-gcm@openssh.com`) `macAlgorithm` reports the negotiated-but-unused MAC-list value — standard SSH semantics, do not treat it as the wire MAC; (2) `.global` promise resolves `.failure` as success-with-value (RTT measurable on refusal — correct for `keepalive@openssh.com` liveness), only connection errors fail the promise; (3) untested-but-documented paths: `.success` reply payload for `.global` requests and empty-allowlist fail-closed negotiation — acceptable residual risk, worth covering when the adapter integrates.
./LOGBOOK.md:802:- IMPLEMENTATION: Bounded generic global requests always set `want-reply`, use the existing protected packet queue, and resolve ordered success/failure replies. `keepalive@openssh.com` is proven by an explicit failure reply (valid liveness), with no TCP-forwarding listener or child channel.
./LOGBOOK.md:809:- BLOCKER: ReluxNIOSSH exposes only TCP-forwarding as a public global request. Its generic reply-requiring request sender and message type are internal, so the adapter cannot send/measure a supported `keepalive@openssh.com` request. Substituting remote TCP forwarding or writing encrypted packets beside the engine is invalid.
./LOGBOOK.md:811:- ROUTING: Product code stopped per TASK-260715-1af33i's explicit insufficient-fork rule. Recommended resume path is a focused TASK-260715-nzdzv3 fork extension for async external signing, generic/specific keepalive reply observation, configurable algorithm allowlists, and an immutable negotiated-algorithm snapshot/event. Full evidence and rejected forced fits are attached as `TASK-260715-1af33i_fork-api-blocker.md`.
./LOGBOOK.md:826:- FINDING: During any KEX (client- or server-initiated) upstream's state machine rejects outbound `channelData` with `protocolViolation` (`SSHConnectionStateMachine.swift:1060-1076`), failing the child write promise. Pre-existing upstream behavior, unchanged by the fork — but automatic byte/time rekey makes the collision window likely under bulk traffic. The NIOSSH adapter (TASK-260715-1af33i) MUST gate/queue child writes on `rekeySnapshot.keyExchangeInProgress` / `NIOSSHRekeyStartedEvent`→`NIOSSHRekeySucceededEvent`; the fork exposes exactly this observability for the contract's "writes MAY suspend" clause.
./LOGBOOK.md:828:- NOTE: `.serverInitiated` rekey reason semantically means "remote-initiated" (a server-role handler labels client-triggered KEX the same way); cosmetic for the client-only tunnel. `requestRekey` in a non-active state fails the promise and also fires `errorCaught` — callers should not treat pre-active requests as recoverable no-ops.
./LOGBOOK.md:830:### 0850 — Minimal ReluxNIOSSH window/rekey fork is reviewable (TASK-260715-nzdzv3)
